  "worked_solution": "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $5$ units *left*, so we have $h=-5.$ The parabola is also translated $3$ units *up*, so we have $k=3.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14241402/assets/25221241ae83ee4bf78c712ea70d8e4cabea2cb569f59be3122412bbb2cae886.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-5,3),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point.",
